window.docContents[52].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Hello___-World___--Summary--Evaluation-vs-Execution":{"contents":"Side effects are aspects of program execution that go beyond the evaluation of mathematical expressions, such as reading files, throwing exceptions, or triggering industrial machinery.\nWhile most languages allow side effects to occur during evaluation, Lean does not.\nInstead, Lean has a type called  that represents descriptions of programs that use side effects.\nThese descriptions are then executed by the language's run-time system, which invokes the Lean expression evaluator to carry out specific computations.\nValues of type  are called  actions.\nThe simplest is , which returns its argument and has no actual side effects.\n\n actions can also be understood as functions that take the whole world as an argument and return a new world in which the side effect has occurred.\nBehind the scenes, the  library ensures that the world is never duplicated, created, or destroyed.\nWhile this model of side effects cannot actually be implemented, as the whole universe is too big to fit in memory, the real world can be represented by a token that is passed around through the program.\n\nAn  action  is executed when the program starts.\n can have one of three types:\n\n*  is used for simple programs that cannot read their command-line arguments and always return exit code ,*  is used for programs without arguments that may signal success or failure, and*  is used for programs that take command-line arguments and signal success or failure.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Hello, World!\u0009Summary","header":"2.6.1. Evaluation vs Execution","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Hello___-World___--Summary--Evaluation-vs-Execution"},"/#hashing":{"contents":"Java and C# have hashCode and GetHashCode methods, respectively, that compute a hash of a value for use in data structures such as hash tables.\nThe Lean equivalent is a type class called :\n\n\n\nIf two values are considered equal according to a  instance for their type, then they should have the same hashes.\nIn other words, if  then .\nIf , then  won't necessarily differ from  (after all, there are infinitely more  values than there are  values), but data structures built on hashing will have better performance if unequal values are likely to have unequal hashes.\nThis is the same expectation as in Java and C#.\n\nThe standard library contains a function  with type  that can be used to combine hashes for different fields for a constructor.\nA reasonable hash function for an inductive datatype can be written by assigning a unique number to each constructor, and then mixing that number with the hashes of each field.\nFor example, a  instance for  can be written:\n\n\n\n instances for polymorphic types can use recursive instance search.\nHashing a  is only possible when  can be hashed:\n\nBinary trees use both recursion and recursive instance search in the implementations of  and :\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes\u0009Standard Classes","header":"3.5.4. Hashing","id":"/#hashing"},"/#monad-as-design-pattern":{"contents":"Each of these examples has consisted of:\n\n* A polymorphic type, such as , , , or * An operator andThen that takes care of some repetitive aspect of sequencing programs that have this type* An operator ok that is (in some sense) the most boring way to use the type* A collection of other operations, such as , , , and , that name ways of using the type\n\nThis style of API is called a monad.\nWhile the idea of monads is derived from a branch of mathematics called category theory, no understanding of category theory is needed in order to use them for programming.\nThe key idea of monads is that each monad encodes a particular kind of side effect using the tools provided by the pure functional language Lean.\nFor example,  represents programs that can fail by returning ,  represents programs that can throw exceptions,  represents programs that accumulate a log while running, and  represents programs with a single mutable variable.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Monads\u0009One API, Many Applications","header":"4.1.5. Monads: A Functional Design Pattern","id":"/#monad-as-design-pattern"},"/#single-branched-if":{"contents":"When working in a monad, a common pattern is to carry out a side effect only if some condition is true.\nFor instance,  contains a check for vowels or consonants, and letters that are neither have no effect on the state.\nThis is captured by having the else branch evaluate to , which has no effects:\n\n\n\nWhen an if is a statement in a do-block, rather than being an expression, then  can simply be omitted, and Lean inserts it automatically.\nThe following definition of  is completely equivalent:\n\n\n\nA program that uses a state monad to count the entries in a list that satisfy some monadic check can be written as follows:\n\n\n\nSimilarly, if not E1 then STMT... can instead be written unless E1 do STMT....\nThe converse of  that counts entries that don't satisfy the monadic check can be written by replacing if with unless:\n\n\n\nUnderstanding single-branched if and unless does not require thinking about monad transformers.\nThey simply replace the missing branch with .\nThe remaining extensions in this section, however, require Lean to automatically rewrite the do-block to add a local transformer on top of the monad that the do-block is written in.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Monad Transformers\u0009More do Features","header":"6.4.1. Single-Branched  if","id":"/#single-branched-if"}});